It is easy to talk about fit-out and FF&E in the abstract. A single guest room makes it
concrete — almost every discipline touches the same 30 square metres.

## The shell

Partitions, ceiling and wet-area waterproofing are fit-out, finished before any furniture
enters the room. The wall finish behind the headboard is often custom — a fabric panel or
veneer chosen to anchor the whole room's palette.

## The fixed pieces

Wardrobes, vanities and headboards are manufactured off-site to the room's exact drawing,
then installed as a single fitted sequence rather than assembled piece by piece on site.

## The loose pieces

Casegoods, seating and the bed itself arrive separately, on their own procurement timeline,
often from a different factory than the fixed joinery — which is why sequencing the
delivery matters as much as sourcing each item.

## The finishing layer

Drapery, carpet, lighting and artwork go in last, in that order, because each one can mark
or damage the layer beneath it if installed out of sequence.

By the time a guest checks in, a dozen supply chains have converged in one room — and none
of them should be visible to the person staying in it.
